ximizes mobility, and control while minimizing the chance for the opponent to exploit.\nAnswer: (C, 3)\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The current game is in the opening phase. The available moves are (C, 3), (C, 5), and (E, 3).\n- **(C, 3)**: This move places a piece on the main diagonal and flips the opponent\'s piece at D4. This breaks the opponent\'s vertical connection of pieces (C4, D4, E4), splitting their group. It results in a diagonal formation of friendly pieces (C3, D4, E5), which is generally more flexible and harder for the opponent to attack than a straight line.\n- **(C, 5)**: This move flips D5 and creates a vertical wall of three pieces (C5, D5, E5). Creating solid walls early in the game is often a strategic error because it gives the opponent stationary targets to flip from the ends (e.g., the opponent could play D6).\n- **(E, 3)**: This move flips E4 and creates a horizontal wall of three pieces (E3, E4, E5). Similar to (C, 5), this reduces mobility and offers the opponent easy flanking opportunities (e.g., the opponent could play F4).\n\nReason: Move (C, 3) is the strategically superior choice. By flipping the central piece D4, it disrupts the opponent\'s connectivity and avoids creating vulnerable "walls" of pieces on the edges of the cluster. It maintains better long-term mobility and shape compared to the other options.\n\nAnswer: (C, 3)'}
